{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 37, :comments ["<b>This is the Torah of the burnt offering.</b> The intent of this verse has not been made known. And in Torat Kohanim they interpreted it to say: just as the ordination offerings had their general rules and precise details stated from Sinai, so too all of them were stated, etc. And I have already written in other places (beginning of parashat Mishpatim) what is to be noted regarding this matter. They further interpreted, of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim; Zevachim 97a), to teach about all the sacrifices that they require a knife like the burnt offering, and to teach about public peace-offerings that they are eaten by males of the priesthood like the meal-offering, etc. And by way of remez it appears following what they said in Menachot (Menachot 110a): Resh Lakish said, what is written ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה״ etc. — whoever engages in the Torah of a burnt offering is as if he offered it, etc. — up to here; we too will speak in this manner." "<b>And beforehand</b> I will preface the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, men of truth who study in the academy of lights, who said that the ultimate purpose of engaging in Torah is to purify the holy sparks that fell and that were wrested from the holiness, and to restore them in complete unification to their former state. And they are two aspects: the first, holy sparks that descended into the world of Tohu for a reason known to Him, and what is grasped by those who know knowledge will suffice for a correct understanding; the second, they are the souls that were wrested, which the wicked one wrested from the first Adam in his sin, when he took great spoils, as is known the vast division of souls which he greatly oppressed. And there is nothing that can act upon it to extract what it has swallowed from its mouth except through the medium of engaging in Torah — it effects every fundamental thing: it raises the holy sparks that fell before the world of Tikkun, and it extracts from Samael what he has swallowed from his mouth, and shatters him and annihilates his power until he vomits up the substance he swallowed. And this is their saying, of blessed memory (Kiddushin 30b): if he encountered, etc. — if he is a stone he is ground down, and if he is iron he shatters." "And in this verse the text revealed the wonders of the Torah and its mighty powers, and said <b>זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה</b> — its benefits that surround its reading: <b>לָעֹלָה</b>, meaning that through its agency the Shekhinah is elevated — which is the aspect of Knesset Yisrael. Second, <b>לַמִּנְחָה</b> — this word bears several interpretations, all of them correct: the language of rest (מנוחה), the language of delight (נחת), and the language of resting/laying (הנחה). And this interpretation is the sod (Chullin 91a): ״Upon me this righteous one will lay his head,״ and it is the sod (Genesis Rabbah ch. 10): when Shabbat comes, rest comes, for upon her the righteous one, the Foundation of the World, lays. And it is the sod (Song of Songs 2:6): ״His left hand is under my head and his right hand embraces me.״ Understand:" "<b>לַחַטָּאת וְלָאָשָׁם.</b> These are the two aspects of the purifications: the first, portions of holiness that descended below from their level, initially in the world of Tohu — corresponding to them He said ״לַחַטָּאת.״ And the second, portions of souls that were wrested from man in his sin — corresponding to them He said <b>לָאָשָׁם</b>, for the Torah benefits these two aspects to purify them from the place of their descent. And not purification alone, but also it establishes them in the place from which they were lacking, which is His saying <b>וְלַמִּלּוּאִים</b> — to fill them in their place. This corresponds to the sparks that descended. And corresponding to the portions of souls that Samael wrested from the first Adam, He said <b>וּלְזֶבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים</b>, following His saying (Psalms 50:23): ״He who slaughters a thank-offering honors Me,״ and they interpreted, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 43b): this refers to one who slaughters his evil inclination. And the slaughter is that he vanquishes it, and in the aspect of vanquishing, he separates from it the aspect of good that it wrested from the first Adam. Now behold, this aspect is a great level — to grow mighty to slaughter one's evil inclination, as David did when he said (Psalms 109:22): ״And my heart is wounded within me.״ And this matter produces a great effect in the aspect we strive for, and there is no attainment for this grasping except through the medium of Torah, as they said (Kiddushin 30b): if this wretch encounters you, drag him to the house of study. And so they said (ibid.): the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I created for you the evil inclination, I created for it its antidote, which is the Torah." "<b>Further,</b> it hints in His saying <b>וּלְזֶבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים</b> that the culmination of the purification will be through the medium of the sacrifice (זבח), as it is written (Isaiah 34:6): ״For a sacrifice (זבח) to YHWH in Bozrah,״ and they said, of blessed memory, that the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to slaughter the Satan. And the meaning of this slaughter is that He will remove from it the aspect that gives it life, which is the holy spark. And with this, the שלמים — all the purifications are completed. And the text informed us that this matter too will be attained through the medium of engaging in Torah, for in the merit of the Torah engagement that the righteous practice in this world, this will come about. And to this they hinted, of blessed memory (Sukkah 52a), in their statement that this slaughter will come about in this manner: the Holy One, blessed be He, from one side, and all the righteous holding the knife from the other side, to slaughter that sacrifice, for through their power was the attainment. From this you have learned: ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה״ — for all these wondrous things. <b>לָעֹלָה לַמִּנְחָה</b> — corresponding to the elevation of the Shekhinah and her unification with the Holy One, blessed be He; <b>לַחַטָּאת וְלָאָשָׁם</b> — to purify the two aspects of the purifications that are the foundation of the world; <b>וְלַמִּלּוּאִים וּלְזֶבַח הַשְּׁלָמִים</b> — to unify the descending sparks, to fill them in their place, and to slaughter one's evil inclination with a slaughter after which there is no existence, and also to stand alive before HaShem to lay hand upon those who seek his harm, to slaughter a sacrifice, ״And YHWH will be King, etc. — YHWH will be One and His name One״ — and this is His saying ״הַשְּׁלָמִים,״ meaning, that all aspects of holiness are completed and unified together." "And its saying <b>which HaShem commanded</b> etc. refers back to the subject, which is the Torah that it mentioned at the beginning of the verse — as though it said, ״This is the Torah which HaShem commanded״ etc. — meaning that one must study Torah on this condition, not for any other purpose beside it. And its saying <b>on the day He commanded</b>, for this matter was said to them at the station of Mount Sinai, as it is written ״in the wilderness of Sinai.״ And its saying <b>to offer their offerings</b> — here is an allusion, with an added explanation of what I have already expounded: that one must offer all the parts of holiness that are attributed to Israel, for they are the gradations of holiness, and all the parts of holiness are attributed to them; and through the medium of Torah they will offer their offerings and all the aspects of holiness will be unified, as mentioned above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:30.913063058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}